// Restored after a bad edit lost 149 annotations; plus T29c/T29d/T19c.
attr[seg_0000:1249]: type = code; name = mouse_event_handler; comment = [[[
    The INT 33h event callback the game installs at startup (AX=0x0c, mask 0x3f). It only
    stores its arguments and returns far: buttons to cs:[11c1], x to cs:[11bd], y to
    cs:[11bf]. Spice86 never invokes it, which is why the pointer UI cannot be driven from
    the harness. FINDINGS.md 6.5.
]]]
attr[seg_0000:072d]: type = code; name = far_target_072d
attr[seg_0941:1299]: type = code; name = far_target_1299
attr[seg_0941:1c31]: type = code; name = far_target_1c31

// The script opcode that loads an asset (T27).
attr[seg_0000:2dbd]: type = code; name = vm_op_load_asset; comment = [[[
    Opcode 0x45 of vm_statement_table. Reads a word asset id with `lodsw`, stores it at
    ss:[0b04], points DX at the NUL-terminated filename that follows inline in the script,
    calls the loader at 78f9, then skips past the name with lodsb/cmp/jnz.
    This is what `45 40 00 "logo.IO" 00` in main.io is -- opcode, id 0x0040, name -- and 64
    is logo.IO's catalogue id (FORMATS 3.5). It is the proof that main.io is a script and
    not a table. FORMATS.md section 7.
]]]
attr[seg_0000:78f9]: type = code; name = load_asset_by_name; comment = [[[
    Called by vm_op_load_asset with DS:DX pointing at a NUL-terminated asset filename.
]]]

// The actual framebuffer write, found by T11p2 after the IP bug was fixed.
attr[seg_0e97:0192]: type = code; name = blit_to_screen; comment = [[[
    `rep movsw` -- the block copy that puts a finished frame into VGA memory. A write
    breakpoint on a viewport pixel lands here, and this is the only one of the four
    reported sites that writes memory at all; the others are a wait loop (seg_0000:3d64),
    a PIC end-of-interrupt (seg_0000:93c4) and a jmp, i.e. wherever the CPU happened to be
    when the stop was reported.
    The game therefore renders offscreen and blits, which matches the far pointer the
    sprite path takes its destination from (ss:[1dbf]).
]]]
attr[seg_0000:3d64]: type = code; name = wait_loop; comment = [[[
    `cmp al, ss:[0c9a] / ja` -- a spin the CPU sits in when idle, which is why the status
    line so often reports 017d:3d64 and why it turns up in stop reports that have nothing
    to do with it.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:0a30]: type = code; name = sprite_mode_dispatch; comment = [[[
    Switches on the sprite's word 0 low byte -- 0x10, 0x12, 0x14, 0x16 or 0 -- read as `mov al,[si]` just above. It selects the pixel-format variant, which is what that byte is for; it is not a colour count.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:0ad1]: type = code; name = expand_4bpp_opaque; comment = [[[
    Expands 4bpp to 8bpp two pixels at a time with NO zero test: `lodsb`, high nibble
    via four `shr`, low nibble via `and 0fh`, `add` the base in BH to each, `stosw`.
    NOT the routine mode 0x10 uses -- sprite_mode_dispatch sends 0x10 to 0b4c, whose
    inner loop at expand_4bpp_masked (0b63) skips zero nibbles. A breakpoint here does
    not fire during gameplay (0 hits in 20s of walking, control 5 hits). FORMATS 3.13
    was written from this routine and generalised to 4bpp; 3.13b corrects it.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:0a2c]: type = code; name = spr_0a2c
attr[seg_0e97:0a37]: type = code; name = spr_0a37
attr[seg_0e97:0a3e]: type = code; name = spr_0a3e
attr[seg_0e97:0a42]: type = code; name = spr_0a42
attr[seg_0e97:0a46]: type = code; name = spr_0a46
attr[seg_0e97:0b4c]: type = code; name = sprite_base_from_word3_m10; comment = [[[
    The mode 0x10 path, and the one nearly every sprite takes: identical to sprite_base_from_word3 -- `mov al,[si+6]` then `mov bh,al` -- with an 8-byte header.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:0aa0]: type = code; name = spr_0aa0
attr[seg_0e97:0a84]: type = code; name = spr_mode_0a84
attr[seg_0e97:0a5b]: type = code; name = spr_mode_0a5b
attr[seg_0e97:0b63]: type = code; name = expand_4bpp_masked; comment = [[[
    The 4bpp expander mode 0x10 actually uses, and it KEYS ZERO NIBBLES:
        lodsb / mov ah,al / shr ah,1 x4 / test ah,ah / jz +7 / add ah,bh / mov es:[di],ah
        inc di / and al,0fh / jz .. / add al,bh / stosb / loop
    The `test` is on the nibble BEFORE the base in BH is added, so a test on the final
    palette index can never fire -- that distinction is the whole of FORMATS 3.13b.
    Skipping is `inc di`/`inc di`, leaving whatever was already on screen.
    Confirmed against VRAM: buste.io's portrait matches 1233/1233 non-zero pixels and
    0 of 1071 zero-nibble pixels (T36b).
    BH comes from `[si+6]`, the LOW BYTE of word 3, used directly as an additive base --
    not (word3>>4)*16. The two agree only because that byte's low nibble is always 0.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:0b40]: type = code; name = sprite_base_zero; comment = [[[
    The mode 0 path: `mov bh, 0`, so the base is zero, and `add si,6` -- a **6-byte** header rather than 8.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:05c0]: type = code; name = viewport_row_loop; comment = [[[
    Top of the viewport's per-row loop. `and bh,bh / je` picks the masked or unmasked
    path, then falls into the 4bpp expanders below. Ends at 05f4 with the row step:
        add si, cs:[002c]   // source advance per row
        add di, cs:[002e]   // destination advance per row
    Those two words are how the viewport "scales": it does not resample, it walks the
    source and destination with independent per-row deltas. Sampled live while walking
    in Dragonia: cs:[002c] = 15, cs:[002e] = 321. A destination step of 321 on a
    320-wide buffer shifts each row one pixel right -- a shear, which is the perspective.
    Found by breaking on writes to the e000 back buffer while turning (T11p); 16 hits
    against a control of 0 at the same sites.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:05e5]: type = code; name = viewport_expand_4bpp_mirrored; comment = [[[
    Right-to-left 4bpp expander: low nibble, `je` to skip zero, `add al,bl`, written to
    es:[di-1] with `dec di`. Masked AND mirrored -- used for horizontally flipped sprites.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:0644]: type = code; name = viewport_expand_4bpp; comment = [[[
    The viewport's own 4bpp expander, and unlike the panel's it has NO zero test:
        lodsb / mov ah,al / shr al,1 x4 / add al,bl / stosb / loop 0657
        0657: mov al,ah / and al,0fh / add al,bl / stosb / loop 0644
    High nibble first, base in BL. Opaque, which fits a full-frame background.
    Distinct from expand_4bpp_masked (0b63, panel) and expand_4bpp_opaque (0ad1).
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:06d5]: type = code; name = viewport_fill_rect; comment = [[[
    `rep stosb / add di,bx / mov cx,dx / dec bp / jnz / ret` -- a rectangle fill with a
    per-row stride in BX. The sky and ground bands.
]]]
attr[seg_0000:1249]: type = code; name = mouse_event_handler; comment = [[[
    The INT 33h event handler the game installs at startup, caught live: during boot
    it calls AX=0x00 reset, 0x0f ratio, 0x04 set position, 0x07/0x08 ranges, then
    **AX=0x0c with ES:DX = 017d:1249** -- this routine. After that it never calls INT
    33h again, which is why a mid-game trace recorded 'the game does not use the
    mouse' (FINDINGS 6.4) -- the wrong window, not a wrong conclusion about the code.
    It does not fire under the harness: 0 hits while injecting moves and clicks,
    against 507 for a control at vm_run. Spice86 never raises IRQ12 for injected
    mouse input, so INT 74h never runs and the driver never reaches this callback
    (T29c2).
]]]
attr[seg_0000:35ae]: type = code; name = ui_menu_draw_loop; comment = [[[
    Runs 4,248 times while the ACTION menu is drawn and **zero** times in an equal idle
    window; the largest mover of any handler for that action. Sits with vm_op_draw_menu
    (opcode 0x4b, 159 calls in the same window), so this is the per-item drawing loop the
    menu opcode drives. Attributed by call-count diff, F1 vs idle (T29c).
]]]
attr[seg_0000:42f5]: type = code; name = ui_click_dispatch; comment = [[[
    Runs on **every** mouse click and never while idle -- 953 calls for a menu-entry click,
    125 for a click on the ATTACK button, 69 for a click on a portrait -- always in lockstep
    with seg_0000:01a8 and seg_0000:0008. Not action-specific: this is the click handling
    path itself. Attributed by call-count diff across four different clicks (T29c).
]]]
attr[seg_0000:7ceb]: type = code; name = map_decompress_inner; comment = [[[
    15,963 calls when MAP is chosen from the ACTION menu, zero while idle, alongside
    seg_0000:7cbf (5,999) and seg_0000:7ce9 (5,272). The 0x7cxx range is the container
    decompression code (FORMATS 3.2), so choosing MAP decompresses an asset rather than
    drawing from something resident. Attributed by call-count diff (T29c).
]]]
attr[seg_0000:9386]: type = code; name = party_member_select; comment = [[[
    Runs only when a character portrait is clicked (28 calls), with seg_0000:9396 (28),
    seg_0000:9375 (26) and seg_0000:8e2b (26). The click changes 3.9% of the screen -- a
    selection highlight, not a new screen. Attributed by call-count diff, portrait click vs
    idle (T29c).
]]]
attr[seg_0000:2ded]: type = code; name = vm_op_declare_entity; comment = [[[
    Opcode 0x46, and a **fixed 36-byte** statement: lodsw / lodsb / mov cx,20h / rep movsb.
    The `rep movsb` takes its source from SI, so 32 bytes of the script stream are an inline
    record copied into a structure at es:[bx+6], with `or es:[bx],40h` setting a flag and a
    pointer written at es:[bx+2]. main.io's first two statements are both this, at offsets
    24 and 60 -- the entry point and the one after it (FORMATS 7.5).
]]]
attr[seg_0000:5713]: type = code; name = vm_op_block_init; comment = [[[
    Opcode 0x29, and **variable length: 5 + 2*count**, where count is its own third operand
    byte. lodsw gives a destination offset, lodsb the count, lodsb one byte, then `inc cx`
    and a loop reading one WORD per further iteration. This is the instruction no
    table-driven disassembler can size, and getting it wrong is what put `vmdis` out of
    alignment: at offset 96 its loop swallows `5a 00` and at 143 it swallows `1a 00`, so
    those bytes are operand data rather than the statements they were being read as
    (FORMATS 7.4).
]]]
attr[seg_0000:4b8f]: type = code; name = vm_op_consequence_event; comment = [[[
    Opcode 0x57. Fires 96 times, and zero while idle, at the moment the party attacks a
    friendly NPC -- the action that produces the full-screen demon frame and resets the
    party to the start position (FINDINGS 4.17). Attributed by call-count diff, attack-on-NPC
    vs idle (T29f). Not seen in any other action measured: menu draw, MAP, portrait select
    or a click on empty ground.
]]]
attr[seg_0000:28a9]: type = code; name = vm_op_attack_swing; comment = [[[
    Opcode 0x15. **7,805 calls** in the window where ATTACK is clicked with a target in
    front, against zero idle -- by far the largest mover of any action measured. It also
    appears at 903 calls in a plain idle-vs-idle baseline elsewhere, so it is not
    combat-exclusive; the attack drives it roughly nine times harder. Attributed by
    call-count diff (T29f).
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:038b]: type = code; name = sprite_blit_ingame; comment = [[[
    **The in-game sprite blitter.** Fires 120 times when a character portrait is clicked
    and 0 times in an equal idle window; also 106 on an ACTION menu draw, 112 on MAP and
    23 on an attack (T40b, call-count diff).
    This corrects two things. T11p recorded it firing 445 times during launcher/title/intro
    and **zero in 30s of walking**, which was read as 'the seg_0e97 family does not draw the
    game'. Both are true and not in conflict: it draws the **UI -- panel, portraits, menus**
    -- and not the **3D viewport**, which has its own routines (FORMATS 3.13d).
    Note it is reached without going through sprite_mode_dispatch (seg_0e97:0a30), which
    takes 0 hits in the same windows, so there are two ways into this sprite code and the
    in-game one bypasses the mode switch.
    Its destination comes from the far pointer at ss:[0bc8]/[0bca].
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:0371]: type = code; name = sprite_dest_compute; comment = [[[
    Turns a sprite's x,y into a destination pointer -- the answer to 'where do on-screen
    positions come from' (T40):
        les di, ss:[1dbf]     // destination base, the offscreen buffer
        mov ax, ss:[0c2e]     // Y
        mov bx, ss:[1dd5]     // stride, bytes per row
        mul bx                // Y * stride
        add di, ax
        add di, ss:[0c2c]     // + X
    So **dest = base + Y*stride + X**, and the coordinates live in two engine variables.
    Read live in game: ss:[1dbf] = 0000:e000 (the back buffer blit_to_screen copies from),
    ss:[1dd5] = 0x0140 = 320.
    Polling ss:[0c2c]/ss:[0c2e] while the party panel redraws yields **x=0 y=147** among
    the samples -- exactly the portrait origin measured byte-for-byte against VRAM in
    T36b/FORMATS 3.13b. Other UI positions seen in the same poll: (0,139), (24,157),
    (0,175), (14,199), (31,152).
    ss:[0c30] is a clip or origin offset subtracted at seg_0e97:038b and 03a3; it read 15
    at the same moment.
]]]
attr[seg_0000:4179]: type = code; name = draw_bbox_reset; comment = [[[
    Resets the draw bounding box: ss:[0c2c]/[0c2e] (min X, min Y) to 0x7fff and
    ss:[0c30]/[0c32] (max X, max Y) to 0x8000 -- +MAX and -MAX, the classic min/max
    initialisation. The `cmp/mov` pairs at 41db and 41f8 are the min updates.
    So ss:[0c2c]/[0c2e] are the top-left of the current draw rectangle, which is why
    sprite_dest_compute (seg_0e97:0371) adds them as the destination origin, and why
    polling them during a panel redraw yields each element's own origin (T40).
]]]
attr[seg_0000:469a]: type = code; name = draw_pos_from_entity; comment = [[[
    **Where layout comes from.** X and Y are read out of a per-entity record and written to
    the draw origin, then clamped to the clip bounds in ss:[0c62]/[0c64]:
        mov cx,[di+0ch] / mov ss:[0c2c],cx    // X
        mov cx,[di+0eh] / mov ss:[0c2e],cx    // Y
    The same pair appears at seg_0000:4768-4772 without the clamp. So a drawable's position
    is **data carried in its own record at +0x0c and +0x0e**, not a constant in the code
    (T40c).
]]]
end
